00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:37.290
Attack surface reduction
in Windows is broader

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:39.330
than just single control.

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:42.330
It's a collection of
capabilities designed

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:44.700
to reduce common attack paths.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:48.480
These include attack
surface reduction rule,

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:52.290
hardware-based isolation,
application control,

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:55.500
exploit protection,
network protection,

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:59.130
Windows Defender
firewall, web protection,

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:04.260
control folder access, and
removable storage protection.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:07.020
As you plan adoption,
Microsoft's guidance

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:10.560
on configuring attack
surface reduction capability

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.050
and which Windows
versions support

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:16.530
which features helps you
choose the right control

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:18.730
for a device fleet.

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:25.380
Now, let's get concrete with
attack surface reduction rule.

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:29.730
Some common examples include
blocking executable content

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:34.800
from e-mail client and webmail,
blocking all Office application

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:38.700
from creating child processes,
blocking Office app

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:43.020
from creating executable
content, blocking Office app

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:48.510
from injecting code into other
processes, blocking execution

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:51.270
of potentially
obfuscated script,

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:55.260
and using advanced protection
against ransomware.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:58.800
Each rule can operate
in different mode.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:04.470
Off, not configured or
disabled, which maps to zero.

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:08.730
Block, which enables the
rule maps to the number one.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:13.650
Audit, mapping to two,
and warm, mapping to six.

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:17.700
That range is useful because
it lets you start with Audit

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:24.420
or Warn to automate impact
before enforcing block broadly.

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:29.580
For deployment, you can use
Microsoft Configuration Manager,

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:33.690
Group Policy, PowerShell,
Microsoft Intune,

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:37.410
or other mobile device
management option.

