In a hands-on demonstration,
we're going to see later on how

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:45.720
to navigate the Endpoint
Manager Admin Center and work

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:48.660
through Endpoint security
policies to configure

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:52.300
and deploy these role modes.

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:58.440
Now that we've covered core
protection and hardening,

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:01.230
let's shift into investigation.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.990
This is where Defender for
Endpoint helps you move

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:08.460
from something looks wrong
to a clear understanding

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:13.830
of what happened, what's
affected, and what to do next.

00:17:13.830 --> 00:17:16.530
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:20.130
to use the device page in
Microsoft Defender for Endpoint,

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:23.850
describe the device forensic
information that Defender

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:25.530
for Endpoint collects,

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:29.100
and explain how
behavioral blocking works

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:32.220
to stop threats based
on what they do,

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:35.350
not just what they look like.

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:40.560
Let's start with the
device inventory.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.980
The device inventory page
shows you a list of devices

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:47.400
in your network where
alerts are generated.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:51.330
By default, the list focuses
on device with alerts seen

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:55.290
in the last 30 days, which
helps you prioritize the most

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:56.880
recent risk.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:58.590
In the current
portal experience,

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:02.280
you'll find this inventory
under the assets section,

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:05.620
specifically in devices.

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:09.690
When you pivot into
a specific device,

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:13.890
Defender for Endpoint gives you
an investigation workspace built

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:16.580
around the context and action.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:18.890
You'll see each device detail,

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:24.170
a set of response action you can
execute, and a series of tabs

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:27.350
such as overview,
alerts, timeline,

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:31.470
security recommendation,
software inventory,

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:37.080
discovered vulnerability, and
missing knowledge base, KBs.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:39.420
You'll also see
the summary cards

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:44.070
that helps you quickly orient
yourself, like active alerts,

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:47.820
logged on user, and a
security assessment,

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:51.720
so you can decide whether you're
dealing with a true incident

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.520
and what your next
step should be.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:58.680
Now let's talk about
behavioral blocking

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.260
and containment on decline.

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:06.270
These capabilities help
identify and stop threats based

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:09.180
on behavior and process trees,

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:13.110
even when the threat has
already started running.

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:17.910
The idea is that next generation
protection, endpoint detection

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:19.890
and response, and Defender

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:23.310
for Endpoint components
work together.

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:27.000
Cloud intelligence and
local sensors combine

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:32.100
so suspicious behavior can be
blocked or contained quickly.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:35.070
If you want an example
focused on this concept,

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:36.960
we'll see a couple of examples

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:42.460
in demos we'll run
later on in this video.

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:47.340
Investigation is
only half the story.

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:49.890
You also need the
ability to act.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:52.770
Next, we'll focus on
the response action

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:56.970
so it can take directly on a
device, along with the option

00:19:56.970 --> 00:20:01.710
to collect forensic data
and access system remotely

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:06.380
when deeper triaging
is required.

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:10.160
After completing this module,
you'll be able to perform action

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:15.050
on the device using Defender for
Endpoint, collect forensic data

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:17.120
to support an investigation,

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:24.080
and access device remotely using
built-in response capabilities.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:25.820
When you're
investigating a device,

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:30.350
Defender for Endpoint gives you
device control so it can respond

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:32.120
without switching tools.

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:35.310
You can perform
action, collect data,

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:38.370
or remotely access the machine.

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:41.850
Common response action
includes managing tags,

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:45.000
initiating an automated
investigation,

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:47.790
starting a live
response section,

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.820
collecting an
investigation package,

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:55.470
running an antivirus scan,
restricting app execution,

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:59.340
isolating the device,
containing the device,

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:01.320
consulting a threat expert,

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:06.100
and reviewing the activity
in the Action Center.

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:11.100
One of the most useful
forensic options is collect an

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:13.170
investigation package.

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:16.140
As part of an
investigation or response,

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:21.030
you can gather a package that
includes things like autorun,

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:25.680
install program, network
connection, prefetch files,

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:30.990
running processes, scheduled
tasks, the security event log,

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:35.480
services, Windows Server,
message block session,

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:40.730
system information, temp
directories, users and groups,

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:47.820
and Defender support log, often
referenced as WD support logs.

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:51.420
This helps you capture a
snapshot of the device state

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:56.200
for deeper analysis and
evidence preservation.

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:00.540
For rapid, hands-on triage,

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:05.070
Live Response gives security
operation team a near-instant

00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:08.970
access to a device through
a remote shell connection.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:12.270
You can use basic command to
inspect what's happening.

00:22:12.270 --> 00:22:17.700
Commands like connection, file
info, persistence, process,

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:22.380
registry, schedule
tasks, and services.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:25.290
When you need deeper
capabilities,

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:29.910
advanced command helps you move
from observation to action.

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:35.460
Commands such as analyze,
get file, run, library,

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:38.310
put file, and remediate.

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:41.820
There's also a supporting
video on Microsoft Defender

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:45.420
for Endpoint Live Response
just over four minutes

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:48.250
that demonstrates
the experience.

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:51.570
Once you've
investigated the device

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:56.040
and taken immediate response
action, the next step is often

00:22:56.040 --> 00:23:01.290
to pivot into the evidence
itself, files, user account,

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:06.870
IP addresses, domains, and URLs,
so you can understand scope,

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:11.470
impact, and potential
attacker infrastructure.

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:15.180
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:23:15.180 --> 00:23:19.440
to investigate file in Defender
for Endpoint, investigate domain

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.610
and IP addresses, and
investigate user account,

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:27.570
so you can correlate device
activity with identities

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:30.790
and external infrastructure.

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:35.820
Let's take a look at
file investigation.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:39.900
When a file is associated with
an alert, suspicious behavior,

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:44.280
or notable event, you can
investigate its detail

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:48.480
to determine whether it
exhibits malicious activity,

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:52.020
identifies the
attacker's motivation,

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:55.830
and understand the potential
scope of the breach.

00:23:55.830 --> 00:23:59.100
This is where you validate
whether the file is truly

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:03.780
harmful, whether it's
widespread, and how it connects

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:05.760
to the larger attack chain.

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:09.390
If you want to see deeper
analysis capability,

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:12.930
there's a supporting video
called the Microsoft Defender

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:17.220
for Endpoint Deep Analysis
and additional guidance

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:20.260
on a responding to file alert.

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:26.010
Identity is often
the real target,

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:29.700
so investigating user
account is critical.

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:31.770
You can identify accounts

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:37.260
which are the most active alerts
commonly shown as users at risk

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:40.380
on dashboards, and
investigate scenarios

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:43.470
like potentially
compromised credentials.

00:24:43.470 --> 00:24:47.610
You can also pivot into a user
account directly from an alert

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:51.840
or device investigation
to look for patterns

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:56.520
such as lateral movement, where
the same user account appears

00:24:56.520 --> 00:25:01.530
across multiple devices, or
suspicious sign-in activity,

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:04.600
such as account abuse.

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:10.320
When an IP address
shows up in telemetry,

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:14.310
investigating it helps you
determine whether it's benign,

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:17.610
suspicious, or
clearly malicious.

00:25:17.610 --> 00:25:23.640
In the IP view, you can examine
where the IP is seen worldwide,

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:28.380
look up reverse DNS names,
review alerts related

00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:32.970
to that IP, check whether the
IP appears in your organization

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:34.950
and understand prevalence.

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:41.230
How often is that IP is
observed in your environment?

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:48.450
Domain and URLs often
connect endpoint behavior

00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:50.940
to attacker infrastructure.

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:56.730
In the URL and domain view,
you can review domain details

00:25:56.730 --> 00:26:00.570
and register contact
information.

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:02.430
See Microsoft's verdict

00:26:02.430 --> 00:26:06.690
and check incident related
to the URL or domain.

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:10.350
You can understand prevalence
within your organization

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:13.560
and see the most
recently observed device

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:18.250
that communicated with
that URL or domain.

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:24.270
Next, we'll focus on automation,
because at enterprise-scale,

00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:27.780
consistent response
depends on smart default,

00:26:27.780 --> 00:26:30.960
controlled remediation,
and integration

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:36.300
that helps enforce policy
without slowing down analysts.

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:38.790
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:41.910
to configure advanced features
in Microsoft Defender

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:45.750
for Endpoint and manage
automation settings,

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:50.460
so investigation and remediation
can be scaled safely

00:26:50.460 --> 00:26:55.840
across different device groups
and operational requirements.

00:26:56.910 --> 00:26:59.850
Here we explore the
advanced feature area,

00:26:59.850 --> 00:27:03.150
which provides a set
of on/off switches

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:06.090
for major feature
within the product.

00:27:06.090 --> 00:27:09.990
In this context, we're focusing
specifically on the settings

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:12.390
that influences automation.

00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:14.880
So you can decide whether
you want the service

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:19.260
to act automatically, where
you want human approval,

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:25.420
and what data source automated
investigation can use.

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:30.870
Automation quality
depends heavily

00:27:30.870 --> 00:27:35.370
on what data automated
investigation can collect

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:37.020
and analyze.

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:40.980
With file content analysis,
you can enable a capability

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:45.150
where certain files and e-mail
attachments are automatically

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:46.650
uploaded to the cloud

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:51.810
for additional inspection during
an automated investigation.

00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:54.030
With memory content analysis,

00:27:54.030 --> 00:27:57.540
you can enable
automated investigation

00:27:57.540 --> 00:28:00.060
of processed memory content.

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:03.900
When this is turned on, memory
content might be uploaded

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:06.990
to Microsoft Defender
for Endpoint as part

00:28:06.990 --> 00:28:10.080
of an automated investigation.

00:28:10.080 --> 00:28:14.190
You can also control what
automation should skip

00:28:14.190 --> 00:28:17.760
by configuring automation
folder exclusion.

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:20.970
This exclusion lets
you specify folder

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:24.390
that automated
investigation will bypass,

00:28:24.390 --> 00:28:26.220
and you can define attributes

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:30.090
such as the folder paths
themselves, file extension,

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:35.260
and file names for fine
tuning what gets excluded.

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:39.330
Now let's take a look at
automated investigation

00:28:39.330 --> 00:28:41.340
and remediation level.

00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:46.140
This is how you decide what
the system can fix on its own

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:49.710
versus what requires
approval from us.

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:51.420
Options include full,

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:55.320
where threats can be
remediated automatically,

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:59.490
and several semi-level
where approval is required,

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:03.810
such as requiring approval
for any remediation,

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:07.980
requiring approval for
curve folders remediation,

00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:13.080
or requiring approval for
non-temp folder remediation.

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:15.960
If you want no
automated response,

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:18.060
you can also set a level

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:22.410
that disables automated
remediation action.

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:25.470
These settings are often
applied by device group,

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:28.830
so you can be more
aggressive where appropriate

00:29:28.830 --> 00:29:32.590
and more cautious on
sensitive systems.

00:29:34.740 --> 00:29:38.400
Here we connect Endpoint
Risk to access control

00:29:38.400 --> 00:29:41.940
by using Microsoft
Defender Endpoint Manager

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:46.860
and conditional access to
block at-risk devices.

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:51.000
The flow starts by turning on
the Microsoft Intune connection

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:54.240
from the Microsoft
Defender XDR portal.

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:58.410
Next, you turn on the Defender
for Endpoint Integration

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:01.200
in the Intune Admin Center.

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:05.310
And then you create a
compliance policy in Intune,

00:30:05.310 --> 00:30:10.140
assign that policy to the right
users or devices, and finally,

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:14.820
create a Microsoft Entra ID
conditional access policy

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:18.600
that can enforce
access decision based

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:21.270
on device compliance or risk.

00:30:21.270 --> 00:30:25.200
There's also a supporting
video on conditional access

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:27.820
with Defender for Endpoint.

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:33.210
Now we'll focus on how alerts
are configured and managed.

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:35.250
Because detection
is only helpful

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:39.360
if the right people are
notified, noise is controlled,

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:42.180
and your indicators
and policies align

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:46.620
to how your
organization operates.

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:48.930
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:30:48.930 --> 00:30:53.190
to configure alert settings in
Microsoft Defender for Endpoint

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:58.110
and manage indicators so you
can improve signal-to-noise

00:30:58.110 --> 00:31:01.560
and enforce blocking or auditing

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:05.200
for known indicators
of compromise.

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:09.930
Let's revisit advanced
feature from a detection

00:31:09.930 --> 00:31:12.540
and investigation perspective.

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:15.390
This area provides
many on/off switch

00:31:15.390 --> 00:31:18.660
that controls how Defender
for Endpoint connects

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:22.380
with other services and
how information is shared

00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:24.870
across the broader
security stack.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:26.160
So your alerting

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:31.170
and investigation
experience is unified.

00:31:31.170 --> 00:31:34.860
Here are several key
integrations you can enable.

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:39.540
Microsoft Defender for Identity
Integration retrieves enriched

00:31:39.540 --> 00:31:43.410
user and device data from
Defender for Identity

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:46.560
and forwards Defender
for Endpoint Signal,

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:51.600
improving correlation across
identities and endpoints.

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:53.190
Sharing endpoint alerts

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:57.330
with Microsoft Compliance Center
supports broader governance

00:31:57.330 --> 00:31:59.940
and compliance workflow.

00:31:59.940 --> 00:32:03.270
Microsoft Defender for Cloud
App can receive Defender

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:07.860
for Endpoint Signal, giving
administrators deeper visibility

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:12.300
into both sanctioned
Cloud Apps and Shadow IT.

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:15.600
The Microsoft Intune
Connector enables sharing

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:20.550
of device information and
stronger policy enforcement.

00:32:20.550 --> 00:32:25.500
Web content filtering can be
enabled to help control access

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:28.530
to risky categories of content.

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:32.460
And Microsoft Secure Score
can receive Defender

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:36.780
for Endpoint Single, so Secure
Score reflects your device's

00:32:36.780 --> 00:32:41.530
security posture and
improvement opportunities.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:45.060
Here we look at how Defender

00:32:45.060 --> 00:32:49.320
for Endpoint can notify the
right people at the right time.

00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:53.280
You can configure the service
to send e-mail notification

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:57.270
to specific recipient whenever
new alerts are generated.

00:32:57.270 --> 00:33:01.230
This ensures that the
appropriate responders are

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:05.130
immediately notified and
can take action based

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:07.500
on alert severity.

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:09.600
Configuration is
restricted to user

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:12.780
with the managed security
settings permission.

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:16.530
In environment using basic
permission management,

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:20.040
this typically includes
security administrator

00:33:20.040 --> 00:33:22.470
or global administrator.

00:33:22.470 --> 00:33:26.550
You can fine-tune which alert
severity level triggers

00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:31.530
notifications and manage the
recipient list over time,

00:33:31.530 --> 00:33:35.460
with new recipients only
receiving alerts generated

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:37.930
after they've been added.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:43.470
Not every alert
represents a real risk,

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:46.260
and alert tuning helps
you reduce noise

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:48.840
without losing visibility.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:53.010
Here, we explore how to create
tuning rules for alerts

00:33:53.010 --> 00:33:56.430
that are known to be benign
in your environment,

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:59.160
such as an internally
developed tool

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:02.340
or a common
administrative process.

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:07.650
You can scope suppression rules
to a specific user, a device,

00:34:07.650 --> 00:34:12.750
a device group, so only matching
alerts are suppressed while

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:15.300
others remain visible.

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:18.570
In scenarios that requires
broader suppression,

00:34:18.570 --> 00:34:22.280
such as an organization-wide
benign tool,

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:27.050
administrators can suppress
alert across all devices.

00:34:27.050 --> 00:34:30.230
This approach helps
analysts stay focused

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:37.030
on meaningful threats instead
of chasing expected activity.

00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:41.760
Indicators of
compromise, or IoC,

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:46.200
are a foundational capability
in endpoint protection.

00:34:46.200 --> 00:34:48.900
Defender for Endpoint
allows security teams

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:54.480
to define indicators and enforce
action, such as allowing,

00:34:54.480 --> 00:34:56.490
auditing, blocking,

00:34:56.490 --> 00:35:01.130
or remediating activities
tied to those indicators.

00:35:01.130 --> 00:35:07.040
You can manage IOCs for
files, IP addresses, URLs,

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:09.810
domains, and certificates.

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:13.800
This enables proactive
blocking of known threats,

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:17.070
consistent enforcement
across devices,

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:21.420
and tighter control during
incident responses.

00:35:21.420 --> 00:35:25.350
There is also a supporting
video on unified IOCs

00:35:25.350 --> 00:35:29.430
that demonstrates how those
indicators work together

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:31.600
across the platform.

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:37.470
With detection and response
covered, we now shift

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:41.430
into understanding and
reducing exposure.

00:35:41.430 --> 00:35:45.870
Microsoft Defender Vulnerability
Management Tool helps you

00:35:45.870 --> 00:35:50.940
continuously identify weaknesses
across your environment,

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:52.500
providing visibility

00:35:52.500 --> 00:35:57.810
into vulnerabilities
before they're exploited.

00:35:57.810 --> 00:36:00.510
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:36:00.510 --> 00:36:04.290
to describe how vulnerability
management works in Defender

00:36:04.290 --> 00:36:08.670
for Endpoint, identity
vulnerabilities in your devices,

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:10.680
and track emerging threats

00:36:10.680 --> 00:36:15.460
so remediation can be
prioritized effectively.

00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:20.040
Defender Vulnerability
Management uses built-in

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:23.730
and agentless scanners
to continuously monitor

00:36:23.730 --> 00:36:28.410
and detect risk, even when
devices aren't connected

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:30.150
to the corporate network.

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:34.350
This capability provides
near real-time insights

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:36.360
into vulnerabilities,

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:40.560
misconfiguration,
and exposure trends.

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:45.360
The goal is to help security
team move from reactive patching

00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:49.010
to proactive risk reduction.

00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:51.950
There's also an interactive
guide available that walks

00:36:51.950 --> 00:36:52.970
through threats

00:36:52.970 --> 00:36:56.760
and vulnerability
management end-to-end.

00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:03.420
Here we explore how
vulnerability data is organized

00:37:03.420 --> 00:37:05.160
and consumed.

00:37:05.160 --> 00:37:09.390
The vulnerability management
navigation pane provides

00:37:09.390 --> 00:37:12.510
multiple views,
including a dashboard

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:17.160
with overall exposure
scores, threat awareness,

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:19.590
and top recommendation.

00:37:19.590 --> 00:37:22.950
You can drill into
security recommendations

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:25.230
to see effective software,

00:37:25.230 --> 00:37:29.280
remediation option,
and threat context.

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:34.170
Remediation view helps track
active fixes and exceptions,

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:37.440
while inventory
provides visibility

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:40.740
into all organizational assets.

00:37:40.740 --> 00:37:45.360
Additional view highlights
known weaknesses, recent events

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:49.020
that impacts risk, and
baseline assessment

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:51.550
for compliance monitoring.

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:55.860
Once risks are identified,

00:37:55.860 --> 00:37:58.650
remediation follows
a clear flow.

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:04.040
You select a recommendation,
submit a remediation request,

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:08.510
and then review progress
as fixes are implemented.

00:38:08.510 --> 00:38:12.060
This structured
approach helps security

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:15.060
and IT team collaborate
effectively

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:19.170
and track risk
reduction over time.

00:38:19.170 --> 00:38:23.800
Now, let's see Defender
for Endpoint in action.

